<p>Your goods are packed, the container is booked, and then the forwarder writes back: <em>"MSDS not acceptable."</em> For Indian exporters of chemicals, cosmetics, paints, oils, fragrances, incense and batteries, this is one of the most common reasons a shipment misses its vessel or flight.</p>
<p>The good news: almost every MSDS rejection comes from a small set of avoidable mistakes. Here are the ten we see most often, and how our experts make sure your MSDS (now called the SDS) is right before your cargo moves.</p>

<h2>Who Checks Your MSDS When You Ship Out of India?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Freight forwarders and customs brokers</strong>, before they accept the booking</li>
<li><strong>Shipping lines</strong>, who decide whether the cargo is dangerous goods and how it is stowed</li>
<li><strong>Airlines and couriers</strong>, who apply the stricter air transport rules</li>
<li><strong>Customs</strong>, at the Indian port and at destination</li>
<li><strong>Your buyer</strong>, who needs it for their own workplace and market compliance</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of them reads the MSDS for something slightly different, so it has to satisfy all of them.</p>

<h2>The 10 Most Common MSDS Mistakes</h2>

<h3>1. An Old, Non-GHS Format</h3>
<p>Many exporters still use an old MSDS with 8 or 9 sections, or one written years ago. Today, forwarders and destination markets expect the <strong>16-section GHS format</strong>. An outdated layout is often rejected on sight. See our guide to <a href="/resources/blogs/msds-16-sections-complete-guide-sds-format-hazard-info">the 16 sections of an MSDS</a>.</p>

<h3>2. Using the Supplier's MSDS for Your Own Product</h3>
<p>If you have blended, diluted, repacked or rebranded a material, your supplier's MSDS no longer describes your product. The MSDS must cover <strong>your product, under your company's name and details</strong>.</p>

<h3>3. Product Name Not Matching the Invoice</h3>
<p>The name on the MSDS must match the commercial invoice, packing list and labels. A brand name on one document and a chemical name on another raises questions at the port.</p>

<h3>4. Missing or Wrong Transport Information (Section 14)</h3>
<p>Section 14 is what the shipping line and airline read first: <strong>UN number, proper shipping name, hazard class, packing group and marine pollutant status</strong>. A blank Section 14, "not available", or a UN number that does not match the product's hazards is the single biggest cause of rejections.</p>

<h3>5. Calling a Product "Non-Hazardous" Without Basis</h3>
<p>Writing "non-hazardous" without a proper classification does not convince a carrier, especially if an ingredient is clearly hazardous. If your product is not dangerous goods, the MSDS must show why, with a classification based on the actual composition.</p>

<h3>6. Incomplete Composition and Missing CAS Numbers</h3>
<p>Section 3 must identify the hazardous ingredients with their CAS numbers and concentrations, as required in your destination market. Vague entries like "proprietary blend" for a hazardous ingredient invite questions. This is why we always ask for the full composition and identify the CAS numbers; read <a href="/resources/blogs/msds-for-export-information-checklist-cas-number-coa">the questions we ask before drafting your MSDS</a>.</p>

<h3>7. Guessed Physical Data</h3>
<p>A missing or estimated flash point, pH or density can change how a product is classified and shipped. A liquid's flash point, for example, decides whether it travels as a flammable liquid. Where it matters, we use your <strong>certificate of analysis</strong> or arrange the test through our <a href="/services/testing/chemicals-product-testing">chemical testing</a> partners.</p>

<h3>8. Wrong Format for the Destination Market</h3>
<p>An MSDS written for India may not satisfy the EU, the US, Canada or China, each of which has its own format, classification rules and language. We always ask <strong>which market you are exporting to</strong> first; see the <a href="/resources/blogs/global-sds-regulatory-map">global SDS regulatory map</a>.</p>

<h3>9. Sea Information Used for an Air Shipment</h3>
<p>The IMDG Code for sea and the IATA Dangerous Goods Regulations for air are different. A product that moves easily by sea can face restrictions or quantity limits by air. Check Section 14 whenever you change the mode of shipment.</p>

<h3>10. Missing Supporting Documents for Batteries</h3>
<p>Lithium batteries and products containing them need a <strong>UN 38.3 test summary</strong> alongside the MSDS. Without it, airlines will not accept the shipment. Read more in <a href="/resources/blogs/msds-export-certification-shipment-cannot-fly">MSDS for exports: the certification your shipment cannot fly without</a>.</p>

<h2>A Quick Pre-Shipment Check</h2>
<table>
